What are the historical origins and significance of Rogation Days in the Christian church?
Rogation Days have their historical origins in the pagan Roman procession known as Robigalia, which was held to propitiate the deity of agricultural disease, Robigus, and ask for protection from wheat rust. The Christian major rogation replaced this pagan practice and was introduced by Mamertus, bishop of Vienne, around AD 470. The minor rogation days were also introduced by Mamertus and were later adopted into the Roman rite by Pope Leo III. The significance of Rogation Days lies in their observance of days of prayer and fasting, processions, and the Litany of the Saints, which reflect the beseeching of God for the appeasement of his anger and for protection from calamities.
