Describe the role of the Y chromosome in male development and how it differs from the X chromosome.
The Y chromosome plays a crucial role in male development by containing the SRY gene, which triggers testis development and sets the developmental pathway towards maleness. In the presence of the SRY gene, the paramesonephric ducts develop into testes, which then release hormones that cause the paramesonephric ducts to regress, leading to the formation of male reproductive organs. In contrast, the X chromosome does not contain the SRY gene and is associated with female development, with the mesonephric ducts regressing and the paramesonephric ducts developing into female reproductive organs.
