Explain how the presence of the Y chromosome leads to the development of male characteristics and reproductive organs.
The presence of the Y chromosome leads to the development of male characteristics and reproductive organs through the action of a single gene called SRY (Sex-Determining Region Y). This gene acts as a signal to set the developmental pathway towards maleness. Specifically, the presence of SRY triggers testis development, which in turn leads to the release of hormones that cause the paramesonephric ducts to develop into male reproductive organs, such as the epididymis, vas deferens, and seminal vesicles. The SRY gene is responsible for initiating the process of virilization, which results in the development of male characteristics and reproductive organs.
