What is the historical context and significance of the Vitruvian Man drawing?
The Vitruvian Man drawing, created by Leonardo da Vinci around 1490, is a study of the proportions of the human body, linking art and science in a single work. It is based on the principles described by the ancient Roman architect Vitruvius in his book "De Architectura" (Book III), which states that a well-designed building should conform to the proportions of the human body. The drawing depicts a man inscribed in a circle and square, representing the ideal proportions of the human form. This work represents Renaissance Humanism, which emphasized the connection between art and science, and has become an iconic representation of the intersection of these two fields.
