What notable literary achievements and controversies did Alexander Pope experience during his prime years (1720-1740), and how did they impact his reputation?
During his prime years (1720-1740), Alexander Pope experienced several notable literary achievements and controversies. 

Notable achievements include:

1. Translating the "Iliad" (1715-20) and the "Odyssey" (1725-26), which are still considered great works of literature.
2. Writing the mock-heroic poems "The Rape of the Lock" (1712-17) and "The Dunciad" (1728-43), which are still considered among the greatest mock-heroic poems ever written.
3. Producing an opulent new edition of Shakespeare (1725), although it was criticized for silently "regularizing" Shakespeare's metre and rewriting his verse in some places.

