The journalist who wrote the book "Extraordinary Popular Delusions and the Madness of Crowds" was Charles Mackay. The book, published in 1841, is an early study of crowd psychology that explores how crowds of people often behave irrationally, particularly in relation to economic bubbles and other forms of mass hysteria. The book specifically examines the rise and fall of tulip mania in the 17th century, among other examples, to illustrate the concept of crowd psychology and the tendency for people to follow irrational trends and fads.
